<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> Targets</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yanming Lai
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author (yanming.lai@polyu.edu.hk)</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Defeng Sun
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Applied Mathematics, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The tremendous success of Transformer models in fields such as large language models and computer vision necessitates a rigorous theoretical investigation. To the best of our knowledge, this paper is the first work proving that standard Transformers can approximate Hölder functions <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0},0&lt;\lambda\leq 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0},0&lt;\lambda\leq 1)</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> distance (<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,\infty]</annotation></semantics></math>) with arbitrary precision. Building upon this approximation result, we demonstrate that standard Transformers achieve the minimax optimal rate in nonparametric regression for Hölder target functions. It is worth mentioning that, by introducing two metrics: the size tuple and the dimension vector, we provide a fine-grained characterization of Transformer structures, which facilitates future research on the generalization and optimization errors of Transformers with different structures. As intermediate results, we also derive the upper bounds for the Lipschitz constant of standard Transformers and their memorization capacity, which may be of independent interest. These findings provide theoretical justification for the powerful capabilities of Transformer models.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the tremendous success of machine learning in various applications has sparked theoretical research in this field. As an important model in machine learning, neural networks are a key area of study. One of the reasons for the powerful success of neural networks is their strong expressive capability; in other words, neural networks can approximate a wide range of function classes with arbitrary precision. As the most fundamental and simple model in neural networks, feedforward neural networks (FNNs) have received the most extensive and mature investigation in this area. This includes studies on both shallow and deep architectures, different activation functions, and the approximation of various function classes, etc.; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> and the references therein. In addition to FNNs, there is also a body of work exploring the approximation capabilities of other types of neural networks, such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, recurrent neural networks (RNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, the memorization capacity of neural networks is also closely related to their approximation ability. Research in this direction can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> and the references therein.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since its introduction by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, the Transformer model has achieved remarkable success across a wide variety of domains, including but not limited to large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Compared to traditional FNNs, Transformers are more efficient due to their use of self-attention mechanisms, which enable parameter sharing and parallel token processing. However, this architecture also introduces challenges in studying the approximation properties of Transformers: they must capture the entire context of each input sequence rather than simply assigning a label to each token independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretical research on the expressive power of Transformers began with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, which introduced the concept of contextual mapping—the ability to distinguish tokens that are in the same sequence but at different positions, or tokens across different input sequences. By demonstrating that Transformers with biased self-attention layers can implement contextual mapping, the authors established a universal approximation theorem for such Transformers regarding continuous functions. This method was later extended to sparse Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> also applied this approach to study constrained Transformers. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> investigated the closely related memorization problem. They refined the techniques in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, enhancing the parameter efficiency of attention layers, and established an upper bound for Transformers solving memorization tasks. Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> improved the upper bounds of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and provided lower bounds. Other studies on the memorization capabilities of Transformers include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Leveraging the properties of the Boltzmann operator, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> proved that a single-layer, single-head self-attention mechanism without bias terms is sufficient to achieve contextual mapping. However, this comes at the cost of a separation parameter that decays exponentially with the size of the dictionary. Building on this result, they also established a universal approximation theorem for continuous functions. Recently, there have been some quantitative works in this area. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> provided a quantitative characterization of the convergence rate when Transformers approximate functions in Hölder space <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the dependence of Transformer size on approximation accuracy.
Based on the Kolmogorov-Arnold Representation Theorem, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> constructed several Transformers that can overcome the curse of dimensionality when approximating functions in Hölder space <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing the activation functions in the feed-forward layers. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> utilized an interpolation-based approach to show that self-attention can approximate the ReLU function. By combining this with classical ReLU FNN approximation results, they proved that two-layer multi-head attention can approximate any continuous function. Additional works regarding the expressive capability of Transformers can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and the references therein. However, in these prior studies, there still does not exist any quantitative characterization result for the approximation of functions in general Hölder space <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> by Transformers, even though <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a central object of study in approximation theory. In other words, no existing work has considered the approximation rate of the standard Transformer when the target function possesses higher-order continuous derivatives. We therefore pose the first question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At what rate can standard Transformers approximate functions in general Hölder space <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a class of non-parametric models, neural networks have been widely used in recent years to solve regression problems. A series of prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> has demonstrated that FNNs with various architectures can achieve the minimax optimal rate in regression tasks. In contrast, research on utilizing Transformers for regression remains relatively limited at present. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> investigated Transformers with infinite-dimensional inputs and derived error bounds for regression under the assumption of anisotropic smoothness in the target function. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> studied error estimation for Transformers using hardmax and ReLU, respectively, as activation functions in the attention layers.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> analyzed the error bounds of standard Transformers for regression problems and obtained a sub-optimal rate under the assumptions of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-continuous target functions and weakly dependent data. None of these studies have answered the second key question we raise:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can standard Transformers achieve the minimax optimal rate when applied to regression problems with <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-continuous targets?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results answer the two questions posed in the previous section. Before stating these results, we first give a brief introduction to the standard Transformer architecture. A Transformer consists of three components: the embedding layer, the feedforward block and the self-attention layer. An embedding layer <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine transformation, defined as</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{W}_{EB}\boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{B}_{EB}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{W}_{EB}\boldsymbol{X}+\boldsymbol{B}_{EB},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{W}_{EB}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times d},\boldsymbol{B}_{EB}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{W}_{EB}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times d},\boldsymbol{B}_{EB}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{EB}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. A feedforward block <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(in)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(in)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> of depth <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is recursviely defined as</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{X};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{X};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sigma_{R}(\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{l-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{l}\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}),\quad l\in\{1,2,\dots,L-1\};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sigma_{R}(\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{l-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{l}\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}),\quad l\in\{1,2,\dots,L-1\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{L-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{L}\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{L-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{L}\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times d_{FF}^{(in)}},\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times W}(l=2,\cdots,L-1),\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}\times W},\boldsymbol{b}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}(l=1,\cdots,L-1),\boldsymbol{b}_{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times d_{FF}^{(in)}},\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times W}(l=2,\cdots,L-1),\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}\times W},\boldsymbol{b}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}(l=1,\cdots,L-1),\boldsymbol{b}_{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the element-wise ReLU function: <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{R}(x)=\max\{x,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{R}(x)=\max\{x,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we say that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated from its feedforward neural network (FNN) counterpart <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}_{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(in)}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}_{FF}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(in)}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{FF}^{(out)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which acts on vectors:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{x};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{x};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sigma_{R}(\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\boldsymbol{f}_{l-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{l}),\quad l\in\{1,2,\dots,L-1\};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sigma_{R}(\boldsymbol{W}_{l}\boldsymbol{f}_{l-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{l}),\quad l\in\{1,2,\dots,L-1\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{FF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{f}_{FF}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\boldsymbol{f}_{L-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{L}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\boldsymbol{W}_{L}\boldsymbol{f}_{L-1}+\boldsymbol{b}_{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A self-attention layer <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> of head number <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and head size <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{X}+\sum_{h=1}^{H}\boldsymbol{W}_{O}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{W}_{V}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{X}\sigma_{S}\left(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{W}_{K}^{(h)\top}\boldsymbol{W}_{Q}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{X}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{X}+\sum_{h=1}^{H}\boldsymbol{W}_{O}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{W}_{V}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{X}\sigma_{S}\left(\boldsymbol{X}^{\top}\boldsymbol{W}_{K}^{(h)\top}\boldsymbol{W}_{Q}^{(h)}\boldsymbol{X}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{W}_{O}^{(h)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times S},\boldsymbol{W}_{V}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{W}_{K}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{W}_{Q}^{(h)}\in\mathbb{R}^{S\times d_{SA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{W}_{O}^{(h)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{SA}\times S},\boldsymbol{W}_{V}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{W}_{K}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{W}_{Q}^{(h)}\in\mathbb{R}^{S\times d_{SA}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the column-wise softmax function: <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{S}(\boldsymbol{x})]_{i}=e^{x_{i}}/\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}e^{x_{j}}\right),i\in[n],\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{S}(\boldsymbol{x})]_{i}=e^{x_{i}}/\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}e^{x_{j}}\right),i\in[n],\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A Transformer <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as an initial embedding layer <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> followed by the alternating composition of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1</annotation></semantics></math> feed-forward blocks <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(k)}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{k+1}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(k)}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{k+1}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> self-attention layers <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(k)}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(k)}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{T}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(K)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(K)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(K-1)}\circ\cdots\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(1)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(1)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(0)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}(\boldsymbol{X})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑻</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{T}(\boldsymbol{X}):=\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(K)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(K)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(K-1)}\circ\cdots\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(1)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{SA}^{(1)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(0)}\circ\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{EB}(\boldsymbol{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We use two metrics to characterize the structure of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math>: the size tuple</p>
<table id="S1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\left(L_{0},W_{0}\right),\left(H_{1},S_{1}\right),\left(L_{1},W_{1}\right),\cdots,\left(H_{K},S_{K}\right),\left(L_{K},W_{K}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\left(L_{0},W_{0}\right),\left(H_{1},S_{1}\right),\left(L_{1},W_{1}\right),\cdots,\left(H_{K},S_{K}\right),\left(L_{K},W_{K}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and the dimension vector</p>
<table id="S1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{d}:=\begin{pmatrix}d_{in}&amp;d_{0}&amp;d_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;d_{K}&amp;d_{out}\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{d}:=\begin{pmatrix}d_{in}&amp;d_{0}&amp;d_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;d_{K}&amp;d_{out}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{EB},B_{FF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{EB},B_{FF}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{SA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{SA}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the upper bounds of the parameters in the embedding layer, all feedforward blocks and all self-attention layers, respectively. And we denote by <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{EB},M_{FF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{EB},M_{FF}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{SA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{SA}</annotation></semantics></math> the total number of the parameters in all feedforward blocks and self-attention layers, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the approximation of Hölder function by Transformers. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a set in <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}=(\mu_{1},\dots,\mu_{d})\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}=(\mu_{1},\dots,\mu_{d})\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a multi-index and denote <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\boldsymbol{\mu}|:=\mu_{1}+\cdots+\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\boldsymbol{\mu}|:=\mu_{1}+\cdots+\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f:=\frac{\partial^{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|}f(\boldsymbol{x})}{\partial x_{1}^{\mu_{1}}\cdots\partial x_{d}^{\mu_{d}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝝁</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f:=\frac{\partial^{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|}f(\boldsymbol{x})}{\partial x_{1}^{\mu_{1}}\cdots\partial x_{d}^{\mu_{d}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the partial derivatives of function <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0},0&lt;\lambda\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0},0&lt;\lambda\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:=s+\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:=s+\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. A function <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the Hölder space <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if it has finite <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, which is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f\|_{C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)}:=\max\left\{\|f\|_{C^{s}(\Omega)},\max_{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|=s}|D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f|_{C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝝁</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f\|_{C^{s,\lambda}(\Omega)}:=\max\left\{\|f\|_{C^{s}(\Omega)},\max_{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|=s}|D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f|_{C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|f\|_{C^{s}(\Omega)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|f\|_{C^{s}(\Omega)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\max_{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|\leq s}\sup_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\Omega}|D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f(\boldsymbol{x})|,\quad|f|_{C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)}:=\sup_{\boldsymbol{x}\neq\boldsymbol{y}\in\Omega}\frac{|f(\boldsymbol{x})-f(\boldsymbol{y})|}{\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{y}\|_{2}^{\lambda}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝝁</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\max_{|\boldsymbol{\mu}|\leq s}\sup_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\Omega}|D^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}f(\boldsymbol{x})|,\quad|f|_{C^{0,\lambda}(\Omega)}:=\sup_{\boldsymbol{x}\neq\boldsymbol{y}\in\Omega}\frac{|f(\boldsymbol{x})-f(\boldsymbol{y})|}{\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{y}\|_{2}^{\lambda}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">In Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we respectively establish approximation results for Hölder functions by Transformers in the <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> norm (<math id="S1.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[1,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[1,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>) and in the <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm. Our results include a quantitative characterization of the Transformer structure. Comparing the two theorems shows that achieving pointwise approximation (<math id="S1.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> approximation) comes at the cost of a larger Transformer size.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq t&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq t&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with components in Hölder space <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Tranformer <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>
with size</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{l}=3dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{l}=3dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/{\gamma}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/{\gamma}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and dimension vector</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1&amp;(2dn+5d)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1&amp;(2dn+5d)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d],q\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d],q\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the weight bounds of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}(1),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-(7dn+\gamma+2)t/\gamma}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}(1),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-(7dn+\gamma+2)t/\gamma}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of parameters of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with components in Hölder space <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Tranformer <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>
with size</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=d3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=d3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{l}=d3^{dn+1}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{l}=d3^{dn+1}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and dimension vector</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1+3^{n}&amp;(2dn+5d)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1+3^{n}&amp;(2dn+5d)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that for <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d],q\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d],q\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{\infty}([0,1]^{d\times n})}\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{\infty}([0,1]^{d\times n})}\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the weight bounds of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}(1),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-\max\{(6dn+2)/\gamma,1/\lambda\}}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}(1),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-\max\{(6dn+2)/\gamma,1/\lambda\}}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of parameters of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We next consider the application of Transformers to nonparametric regression:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{Y}_{i}=f_{0}(\boldsymbol{X}_{i})+\xi_{i},\quad i\in[m]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{Y}_{i}=f_{0}(\boldsymbol{X}_{i})+\xi_{i},\quad i\in[m].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(\boldsymbol{x})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(\boldsymbol{x})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown target function, <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\boldsymbol{X}_{i},Y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}\subset[0,1]^{d\times n}\times\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\boldsymbol{X}_{i},Y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}\subset[0,1]^{d\times n}\times\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are observation pairs, <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. Gaussian noises with <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\xi_{i}=0,\mathrm{Var}(\xi_{i})=\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\xi_{i}=0,\mathrm{Var}(\xi_{i})=\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be the marginal distribution of <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y|\leq B_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y|\leq B_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with some constant <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Y}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Y}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to estimate <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the given observation pairs <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\boldsymbol{X}_{i},Y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\boldsymbol{X}_{i},Y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we consider the following least square problem over a function class <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{f}=\arg\min_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}[f(X_{i})-Y_{i}]^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{f}=\arg\min_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}[f(X_{i})-Y_{i}]^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">The function class <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> can be selected in various forms, such as reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces, polynomial spaces, spline spaces, and neural network function classes. In this paper, we choose</p>
<table id="S1.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}:=f_{tr,B_{Y}}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}:=f_{tr,B_{Y}}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{tr,B_{Y}}(x):=\max\{\min\{x,B_{Y}\},-B_{Y}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{tr,B_{Y}}(x):=\max\{\min\{x,B_{Y}\},-B_{Y}\}</annotation></semantics></math> being a truncation function and <math id="S1.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> being a function class generated by Transformers:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d}):=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathcal{T}}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d}):=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{f_{\boldsymbol{T}}:f_{\boldsymbol{T}}=\left\langle\boldsymbol{T},\boldsymbol{E}_{11}\right\rangle,\boldsymbol{T}\in\mathcal{T}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d})\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑻</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{f_{\boldsymbol{T}}:f_{\boldsymbol{T}}=\left\langle\boldsymbol{T},\boldsymbol{E}_{11}\right\rangle,\boldsymbol{T}\in\mathcal{T}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d})\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S1.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d}):=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(K,L,W,H,S,M_{FF},M_{SA},B_{FF},B_{SA},\boldsymbol{d}):=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\boldsymbol{T}:\boldsymbol{T}\text{ is a Transformer defined by \eqref{Transformer} with }\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝑻</mi><mrow><mtext> is a Transformer defined by (</mtext><mtext><a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></mtext><mtext>) with </mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\boldsymbol{T}:\boldsymbol{T}\text{ is a Transformer defined by \eqref{Transformer} with }\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left.\ L:=\max_{k=0,1,\cdots,K}L_{k},\ W:=\max_{k=0,1,\cdots,K}W_{k},\ H:=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}H_{k},\ S:=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}S_{k}\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>:=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.\ L:=\max_{k=0,1,\cdots,K}L_{k},\ W:=\max_{k=0,1,\cdots,K}W_{k},\ H:=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}H_{k},\ S:=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}S_{k}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix inner product and <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{E}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{E}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix that the entry in the first row and first column is <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, while all other entries are <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{E}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{E}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced by any other fixed matrix, as there is no essential difference here. Our next theorem provides an upper bound of the excess risk <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right\|_{L^{2}(\mu)}^{2}:=\int_{[0,1]^{d\times n}}\left|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right|^{2}d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right\|_{L^{2}(\mu)}^{2}:=\int_{[0,1]^{d\times n}}\left|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right|^{2}d\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, which examines the distance between the estimator <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}\in C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}\in C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the parameters in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) be</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K=n,\ L=\mathcal{O}\left(\log m\right),\ W=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),\ H=\mathcal{O}(1),\ S=\mathcal{O}(1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K=n,\ L=\mathcal{O}\left(\log m\right),\ W=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),\ H=\mathcal{O}(1),\ S=\mathcal{O}(1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{\max\{6dn+2,\gamma/\lambda\}/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log m\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),B_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(m^{\max\{6dn+2,\gamma/\lambda\}/(2\gamma+dn)}\right),B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log m\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{d}=\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1+3^{n}&amp;(2dn+5d)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{d}=\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1+3^{n}&amp;(2dn+5d)3^{dn}C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2\exp\left(-m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2\exp\left(-m^{dn/(2\gamma+dn)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, there holds</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right\|_{L^{2}(\mu)}^{2}\lesssim m^{-2\gamma/(2\gamma+dn)}(\log m)^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\widehat{f}-f_{0}\right\|_{L^{2}(\mu)}^{2}\lesssim m^{-2\gamma/(2\gamma+dn)}(\log m)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the classical work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, the minimax optimal convergence rate for nonparametric regression with <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> targets is <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta(m^{-2\gamma/(2\gamma+dn)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(m^{-2\gamma/(2\gamma+dn)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, ignoring logarithmic factors, our result shows that Transformers achieve minimax optimal rate in the nonparametric regression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Our Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Summarizing the content of the previous section, our contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to derive the approximation rates of standard Transformers for functions in Hölder spaces <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Our results generalize those of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, which studied approximation rates of standard Transformers for functions in <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that higher smoothness of the target function leads to improved approximation rates for the Transformer. Given the fundamental importance of Hölder spaces in the study of partial differential equations and dynamical systems, our results lay a theoretical foundation for investigating the application of Transformers in these areas.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that standard Transformers can achieve the minimax optimal rate under the classical nonparametric regression setting (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). As an intermediate result, we estimate the Lipschitz constant of Transformers (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 2.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), a result that may be of independent interest.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As an intermediate step during the derivation of the approximation results, we obtain a memorization result for the standard Transformer (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma7" title="Lemma 7. ‣ 3.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ 3 Proof of Proposition : Construction of Transformers ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). This improves upon the results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which investigated Transformers with bias terms in the attention layers. This finding may be of independent interest.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous works either lacked a precise characterization of the Transformer architecture or provide only a coarse structural description. In contrast, by introducing two metrics: the size tuple and the dimension vector, we achieve a fine-grained characterization of Transformer structures. This facilitates the analysis of both generalization error and optimization error for Transformers with varying structures in future work.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Organization of This Paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we present the proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> along with the intermediate results needed in the proofs. Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 Proof of Proposition : Construction of Transformers ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–<a href="#S7" title="7 Proof of Lemma : Estimation of Lipschitz Constant ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> contain the proofs of these intermediate results. Finally, we summarize the paper in Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Conclusions ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proofs of Main Results</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in Sections <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, respectively, along with the intermediate results required for these proofs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we first construct a Transformer that can approximate the target function to arbitrary precision over the regions</p>
<table id="S2.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega_{\boldsymbol{\beta}}:=\left\{\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}:X_{ik}\in\left[\frac{\beta_{ik}}{K},\frac{\beta_{ik}+1-\delta}{K}\right),i\in[d],k\in[n]\right\},\quad\boldsymbol{\beta}\in\{0,1,\cdots,K-1\}^{d\times n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega_{\boldsymbol{\beta}}:=\left\{\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}:X_{ik}\in\left[\frac{\beta_{ik}}{K},\frac{\beta_{ik}+1-\delta}{K}\right),i\in[d],k\in[n]\right\},\quad\boldsymbol{\beta}\in\{0,1,\cdots,K-1\}^{d\times n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathbb{N}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We sort the index set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,\cdots,K-1\}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,\cdots,K-1\}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\beta}_{K^{dn}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{\beta}_{K^{dn}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> simply as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> hereafter. The complement of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega^{(flaw)}:=\left\{\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}:\text{there exist }i\in[d],j\in[n],k\in[K]\text{ such that }X_{ij}\in\left[\frac{k-\delta}{K},\frac{k}{K}\right)\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>there exist </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext> such that </mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega^{(flaw)}:=\left\{\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}:\text{there exist }i\in[d],j\in[n],k\in[K]\text{ such that }X_{ij}\in\left[\frac{k-\delta}{K},\frac{k}{K}\right)\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such a Transformer is given in Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> below. We provide a detailed characterization of its structure and derive an upper bound on its magnitude on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{(flaw)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{(flaw)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is deferred to Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Proof of Proposition : Construction of Transformers ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}:[0,1]^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with components in Hölder space <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{s,\lambda}\left([0,1]^{d\times n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Tranformer <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>
with size</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{0}=4,\quad W_{0}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{1}=(3d+1)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{1}=\max\{dn,3\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{l}=3,\quad W_{l}=dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}(4n+11);\quad l=1,\cdots,n-1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{l}=3dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{l}=3dC_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1},\quad S_{l}=3;\quad l=2,\cdots,n;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right),\quad W_{n}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and dimension vector</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1&amp;(2dn+5d)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;d+n+1&amp;(2dn+5d)C_{s+dn-1}^{dn-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{1\times n}&amp;d\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that for <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d],q\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d],q\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in[K^{dn}]}\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in[K^{dn}]}\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})\right|\lesssim\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)/\gamma}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≲</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})\right|\lesssim\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)/\gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here the granularity <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K=\Theta\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Theta\left(\epsilon^{-1/\gamma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the weight bounds of <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),B_{FF}=\max\left\{C(\boldsymbol{f},d,n,s)\epsilon^{-(6dn+2)/\gamma},1/\delta\right\},B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),B_{FF}=\max\left\{C(\boldsymbol{f},d,n,s)\epsilon^{-(6dn+2)/\gamma},1/\delta\right\},B_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(\log\frac{1}{\epsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of parameters of <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{EB}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right),M_{FF}=\mathcal{O}\left(\epsilon^{-dn/\gamma}\right),M_{SA}=\mathcal{O}\left(1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in hand, we are able to prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We divide the integral into two parts:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}^{t}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}^{t}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}+\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}+\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">For the region <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by replacing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\epsilon}{2^{1/t}(1-\delta)^{dn/t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\epsilon}{2^{1/t}(1-\delta)^{dn/t}}</annotation></semantics></math> therein and obtain</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\bigcup_{j\in\left[K^{dn}\right]}\Omega_{j}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2(1-\delta)^{dn}}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{K^{dn}}|\Omega_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2(1-\delta)^{dn}}\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{K^{dn}}|\Omega_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2(1-\delta)^{dn}}\cdot K^{dn}\left(\frac{1-\delta}{K}\right)^{dn}=\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2(1-\delta)^{dn}}\cdot K^{dn}\left(\frac{1-\delta}{K}\right)^{dn}=\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">According to Bernoulli’s inequality, we have the following estimate for the measure of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{(flaw)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{(flaw)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\Omega^{(flaw)}\right|=1-\sum_{j=1}^{K^{dn}}|\Omega_{j}|=1-K^{dn}\left(\frac{1-\delta}{K}\right)^{dn}=1-(1-\delta)^{dn}\leq dn\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\Omega^{(flaw)}\right|=1-\sum_{j=1}^{K^{dn}}|\Omega_{j}|=1-K^{dn}\left(\frac{1-\delta}{K}\right)^{dn}=1-(1-\delta)^{dn}\leq dn\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Using the above estimate and Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we derive that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex54.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim\delta(1-\delta)^{dn(7dn+2)/\gamma}\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)t/\gamma}\lesssim\delta\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)t/\gamma}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≲</mo><mi>δ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≲</mo><mi>δ</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim\delta(1-\delta)^{dn(7dn+2)/\gamma}\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)t/\gamma}\lesssim\delta\epsilon^{-(7dn+2)t/\gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">Choosing</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta\asymp\epsilon^{(7dn+\gamma+2)t/\gamma}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta\asymp\epsilon^{(7dn+\gamma+2)t/\gamma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">we can make</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\boldsymbol{X}\in\Omega^{(flaw)}}|T_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})-f_{pq}(\boldsymbol{X})|^{t}d\boldsymbol{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{\epsilon^{t}}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|T_{pq}-f_{pq}\right\|_{L^{t}([0,1]^{d\times n})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1.2 Main Results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> relies on the horizontal shift technique, first proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> to prove the approximation capabilities of ReLU feedforward neural networks. Specifically, by exploiting properties of the middle value function, this technique strengthens approximation results on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times n}\setminus\Omega^{({flaw})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times n}\setminus\Omega^{({flaw})}</annotation></semantics></math> to the full domain <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, at the cost of a larger network size. The technique is presented in the two lemmas below, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{f}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{f}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the modulus of continuity of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in C([0,1]^{d\times n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in C([0,1]^{d\times n})</annotation></semantics></math>, defiend as</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{f}(\delta):=\sup\bigl\{|f(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{Y})|:\|\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{Y}\|_{F}\leq\delta,\;\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{Y}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}\bigr\},\qquad\text{for any }\delta\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒀</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>for any </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{f}(\delta):=\sup\bigl\{|f(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{Y})|:\|\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{Y}\|_{F}\leq\delta,\;\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{Y}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}\bigr\},\qquad\text{for any }\delta\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, Lemma 3.1)<span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a ReLU FNN function <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{FF}^{(mid)}:\mathbb{R}^{3}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{FF}^{(mid)}:\mathbb{R}^{3}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with width <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, depth <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and weight bound <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> that outputs the middle value of the components of <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, Lemma 3.4)<span id="Thmlemma2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathbb{N}_{&gt;0},\epsilon,\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathbb{N}_{&gt;0},\epsilon,\delta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq\frac{1}{3K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq\frac{1}{3K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume the target function <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in C([0,1]^{d\times n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in C([0,1]^{d\times n})</annotation></semantics></math> and there exists <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}\setminus\Omega^{(flaw)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}\setminus\Omega^{(flaw)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|g(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}\in[0,1]^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\phi(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon+dn\cdot\omega_{f}(\delta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\phi(\boldsymbol{X})-f(\boldsymbol{X})|\leq\epsilon+dn\cdot\omega_{f}(\delta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:=\phi_{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:=\phi_{dn}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by induction through</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}(\boldsymbol{X}):=f_{FF}^{(mid)}\bigl(\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}-\delta\boldsymbol{E}_{i}),\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}),\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}+\delta\boldsymbol{E}_{i})\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}(\boldsymbol{X}):=f_{FF}^{(mid)}\bigl(\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}-\delta\boldsymbol{E}_{i}),\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}),\phi_{i-1}(\boldsymbol{X}+\delta\boldsymbol{E}_{i})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[dn]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[dn]</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{E}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{E}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> being the matrix whose entry is 1 at the position <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\lceil i/n\rceil,i-n(\lceil i/n\rceil-1)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\lceil i/n\rceil,i-n(\lceil i/n\rceil-1)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> elsewhere.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we extend the horizontal shift technique from FNNs to Transformers, thereby establishing approximation in the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm. This extension relies on the parallelizability of Transformers, which is formalized in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3. ‣ 2.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ 2 Proofs of Main Results ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. This lemma will also be used frequently in the later technical proofs. Its proof is deferred to Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of Lemma : Parallelization of Transformers ‣ Standard Transformers Achieve the Minimax Rate in Nonparametric Regression with 𝐶^{𝑠,𝜆} Targets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(1)}:\mathbb{R}^{d^{(1)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{\bar{d}^{(1)}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(1)}:\mathbb{R}^{d^{(1)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{\bar{d}^{(1)}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(2)}:\mathbb{R}^{d^{(2)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{\bar{d}^{(2)}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(2)}:\mathbb{R}^{d^{(2)}\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{\bar{d}^{(2)}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be two feedforward blocks, both with depth </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, widths </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, weight bounds </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, respectively. There exists a feedforward block </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(prl)}:\mathbb{R}^{(d^{(1)}+d^{(2)})\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{(\bar{d}^{(1)}+\bar{d}^{(2)})\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓕</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{F}}_{FF}^{(prl)}:\mathbb{R}^{(d^{(1)}+d^{(2)})\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{(\bar{d}^{(1)}+\bar{d}^{(2)})\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with depth </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, width not greater then </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{(1)}+W^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{(1)}+W^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and weight bound </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{B^{(1)},B^{(2)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{B^{(1)},B^{(2)}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that for any </span><math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{(1)}\times n},\boldsymbol{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{(2)}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{(1)}\times n},\boldsymbol{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{(2)}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
